.Dd July 10, 2026
.Dt XTC_PG 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_pg_join ,
.Nm xtc_pg_leave ,
.Nm xtc_pg_members ,
.Nm xtc_pg_send
.Nd process groups (pub/sub and fan-out)
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libxtc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc_pg.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_pg_join "xtc_reg_t *reg" "const char *group" "xtc_pid_t pid"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_pg_leave "xtc_reg_t *reg" "const char *group" "xtc_pid_t pid"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_pg_members "xtc_reg_t *reg" "const char *group" "int (*fn)(xtc_pid_t, void *)" "void *user"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_pg_send "xtc_reg_t *reg" "const char *group" "const void *msg" "size_t size"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A process group is a named membership set of pids -- the Erlang
.Ql pg ,
Phoenix
.Ql PubSub ,
and Discord-presence pattern.  A group is a duplicate-key entry in an
.Xr xtc_reg 3
registry
.Pq see Fn xtc_reg_register_dup ,
so many pids share one key.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_pg_join
adds
.Fa pid
to
.Fa group
(idempotent -- a pid already in the group is not added twice).  A
process typically joins itself with
.Fn xtc_self .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_pg_leave
removes
.Fa pid
from
.Fa group ,
returning
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
if it was not a member.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_pg_members
visits every member pid.  The callback runs UNDER the registry lock, so
it must be brief and must not re-enter the registry; copy pids out if
more work is needed.  A nonzero callback return stops the walk.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_pg_send
broadcasts
.Fa msg
(a copy) to every current member.  It collects the member pids under the
registry lock and then sends OUTSIDE the lock, so a bounded-mailbox park
never happens with the registry lock held.  A send to an
already-dead member fails harmlessly and is skipped.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_pg_join
and
.Fn xtc_pg_leave
return
.Dv XTC_OK
on success or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code.
.Fn xtc_pg_members
returns the number of members visited.
.Fn xtc_pg_send
returns the number of members the message was successfully sent to.
.Sh NOTES
Single node only.  Cross-node process groups need the distributed
module, which is not built.  Until the registry gains a monitor that
auto-unregisters a member on its DOWN, a crashed member lingers in the
group until something calls
.Fn xtc_pg_leave
for it; a stale-member
.Fn xtc_pg_send
is a harmless no-op.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_reg_t *r = xtc_app_registry(app);

/* Each subscriber joins the topic. */
xtc_pg_join(r, "chat:lobby", xtc_self());

/* A publisher fans a message out to all subscribers. */
int n = xtc_pg_send(r, "chat:lobby", line, len);
/* n = number of subscribers that received it */
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_reg 3 ,
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_app 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
